Change(genesis='66602cd0dec37d58d03eec9518ca805a7ac38ea912d2b0c366a0b67b4b9f50b6', deps=('44ed500dc3fcf117e7b7210bc165688ee6070c4f00d3457faff7b7aa535bc404',), event=Revised(decision='publication', at=datetime(2026, 11, 20, 0, 0, 0), actor='assistant', title='Code is published to suzatary-core one row per landed commit, and every push to core passes one check', body="*How code flows* (2026-10-07): develop HERE, in this private repository (it holds the reports and the history); the merge gate judges a tree, its `public-tree` step building HEAD's public tree on its own as the roots run would; `train.sh land` moves master, then PUBLISHES (the train's tool, `train publish`): the public tree of the landed commit (`scripts/public-tree.sh <out> <rev>`, a function of the commit's tree, its leak guard passed whole, `--check --strict`) committed to suzatary-core's `master` (this checkout's remote `core`) as one commit whose `Source:` trailer names the landed commit, pushed with the train's own credential. That branch is a table of one function along master's first-parent chain: a source is published once (`train.sh publish [<rev>]` again is a no-op), a commit is made of its source and parent alone, and the table only climbs, never rewritten (SECURITY.md states the one exception, a leak, the owner's call). A path no row has held is held until `train.sh publish --admit <path>`, at a terminal, names it in the row's `Admitted:` trailer. A gate branch is a publication too (GitHub serves a deleted branch's commit by its name, and forks share objects), so every push to core passes one check, the train's `publishable`: the leak guard, then what the tree holds against what core's rows already hold. A row of master holds a new path or a new admission only once a person admits it; a gate branch holds new plain-text paths, which pass both checks wherever they go, and never a new admission (a manifest line, a `.gitleaksignore` fingerprint, a change of the secret rules). A check that could not run is no verdict, neither a leak nor a pass. Every deployment, this box included, runs suzatary-core unchanged, pinned by its user repository's flake (private while the repository is): `deploy.sh <rev>` pins a commit of the declared branch's first-parent chain as it is, or the publication whose trailer names the SOURCE commit land printed, and refuses any other rev, naming both lookups (`--ref <branch>` for a fix not yet landed). A publish walks every landed commit after the newest published, so one a land could not publish is published by the next; a landed commit whose tree can never pass (a term added to the lists since) is passed over only by `publish --skip <sha>`, named by the next row's `Skipped:` trailer, and deploy refuses it by name. The live half's `public-check` runs the same `--check --strict` on HEAD, so a leaking commit never lands.", rationale="Decided 2026-10-07. Revised 2026-11-20 (typedtooling T2): a gate branch's commit stays served by its name after the branch is deleted, so the gate's push is a publication, and the leak guard alone read the tree's own admitted files: a pull request that added an opaque file with its own manifest line reached a gate branch.", supersedes=()))